I have just seen a man whom I met a little while ago in the train. He was very polite to me, but when he spoke to me it seemed as if he were mocking me; and when at last I saw him again, he was looking as though he were angry with me, although I did not know why. I do not know how long it is since we met, but it was some time before the train started after that. When the train had gone some distance, and the man had turned his back upon me, I walked slowly behind him; and as soon as I had got as far as I could without being noticed by him, I began to cry out in a loud voice; for, indeed, the man had taken it into his head that I was mocking him. And, as I cried, I ran as fast as I could along the train, so that it was a wonder how I managed to keep my breath. But I am very glad that I did not stop until I had got beyond the man; for then I thought that I should never be able to get away from him.
And now I will tell you how the man behaved. For, although he did not take notice of me for a long while after he had turned his back upon me, he did begin to look at me in a very strange manner; and at last he actually came back to me, and said in a very loud voice that he would soon make me pay dearly for having mocked him. And, indeed, he did seem quite determined to do this. But I did not let him catch me; for I had learned by this time that it was no use trying to reason with such men. I did not try to do anything but run away as fast as I could. So I ran all the way home, and there I sat down on the doorstep to think about what had happened.
But, oh! It is a long time since I had such an adventure as this;
